s a mark for each quantitative observation, preserving individual values while revealing clusters, gaps, and shape. The other displays are designed for categorical data."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.5","skill":"4.A","q":"A histogram of waiting times has most observations between 2 and 8 minutes, with a long tail extending to 35 minutes. Which description best matches the shape?","correct":"Right-skewed","distractors":["Left-skewed","Approximately uniform","Approximately symmetric and bimodal"],"e":"A long tail toward larger values indicates right skew. The direction of skew is named for the tail, not for the side containing most observations."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.6","skill":"4.C","q":"A distribution of household incomes has one clear peak, a median of $62,000, and a few very large values above $500,000. Which description is most defensible?","correct":"Unimodal and right-skewed, with high-income values that may be outliers","distractors":["Unimodal and left-skewed because the median is below the largest values","Symmetric because there is only one peak","Bimodal because the high-income values form a second distribution by definition"],"e":"A small number of unusually large values creates a right tail and may create high outliers. One dominant peak is consistent with a unimodal distribution; a second mode is not implied by a few extremes."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.6","skill":"4.A","q":"A dotplot of machine fill weights shows two separated clusters centered near 498 g and 503 g. What feature should be reported before summarizing the data with one mean and one standard deviation?","correct":"The apparent bimodality, because one center and spread may hide two production regimes","distractors":["The data must be normal because both clusters are narrow","The range alone, because shape is irrelevant once the sample size is large","The median must equal 500.5 g because there are two visible clusters"],"e":"Two distinct clusters suggest different underlying conditions or subgroups. A single mean and standard deviation can obscure that structure, so the shape should be described and investigated."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.7","skill":"3.B","q":"Five delivery times, in minutes, are 18, 20, 21, 21, and 30. What is the mean delivery time?","correct":"22 minutes","distractors":["21 minutes, the median","21.5 minutes, the midpoint of the middle values","30 minutes, the largest observed value"],"e":"The mean is (18 + 20 + 21 + 21 + 30)/5 = 110/5 = 22 minutes. The median is 21 minutes, illustrating the mean's sensitivity to the larger value."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.7","skill":"4.A","q":"A data set contains a strong high outlier. Which pair of summaries is generally more resistant to that outlier?","correct":"Median and interquartile range","distractors":["Mean and standard deviation computed from the original untransformed observations","Mean and range","Median and standard deviation"],"e":"The median and IQR depend primarily on ranks and the middle half of the data, so a single extreme value usually changes them less than it changes the mean, standard deviation, or range."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.8","skill":"4.A","q":"In a boxplot, Q1 = 12, the median = 18, and Q3 = 27. Using the 1.5×IQR rule, which value would be flagged as a high outlier?","correct":"52","distractors":["35","40","Q3 + 1.5(IQR) = 49.5"],"e":"The IQR is 27 − 12 = 15, so the upper fence is 27 + 1.5(15) = 49.5. Of the listed values, only 52 exceeds the upper fence."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.8","skill":"4.A","q":"Two boxplots have the same median, but Group A has a much larger IQR than Group B. Which conclusion is justified from the boxplots?","correct":"The middle 50% of Group A is more spread out than the middle 50% of Group B.","distractors":["Group A has a larger mean than Group B.","Group A has a larger standard deviation than Group B in every possible data set.","Exactly 75% of Group A values exceed the median of Group B."],"e":"The IQR is the distance from Q1 to Q3 and therefore measures spread of the middle 50%. Boxplots alone do not determine means or guarantee a particular standard-deviation ordering."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.9","skill":"4.C","q":"A runner's time is 68 seconds in a population with mean 72 and standard deviation 2, while a swimmer's time is 55 seconds in a population with mean 60 and standard deviation 4. Which performance is farther below its population mean in standard-deviation units?","correct":"The run